// ALG1 Unit 06 Lesson 07 Guided Practice
// Lesson: Multi-Step Algebra Modeling
// MLA Standards: MLA.A1.DF.6; MLA.A1.DF.8; MLA.A1.DF.9

::ALG1_U06_L07_GP_Q01::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L07_GP_Q01] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.6]
A model predicts 50 students will attend. The actual attendance is 54. What is the residual?
{
~A. -4#This subtracts actual from predicted. This course uses actual minus predicted.
=B. 4#Correct. Residual = actual - predicted = 54 - 50 = 4.
~C. 104#This adds the two values instead of finding prediction error.
~D. 50#This is the predicted value, not the residual.




}

::ALG1_U06_L07_GP_Q02::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L07_GP_Q02] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.8]
Which survey method is most likely to produce a representative sample of a high school?
{
~A. Survey only students in the chess club#This sample may overrepresent one group of students.
~B. Survey only students arriving before 7:00 a.m.#This may miss students with different schedules.
~C. Survey only seniors during lunch#This does not represent all grade levels.
=D. Randomly survey students from every grade level#Correct. Random selection across grade levels is more likely to represent the school.




}

::ALG1_U06_L07_GP_Q03::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L07_GP_Q03] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.9]
In a two-way table, 16 students chose art and 24 students chose music. What is the row total for those choices?
{
=A. 40#Correct. Add the counts in the row: 16 + 24 = 40.
~B. 24#This includes only the music count, not the row total.
~C. 8#This subtracts the counts instead of adding them.
~D. 384#This multiplies the counts instead of totaling them.




}

::ALG1_U06_L07_GP_Q04::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L07_GP_Q04] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.6]
A residual is -6. What does this mean?
{
~A. The model predicted exactly correctly#A residual of 0 would mean an exact prediction.
~B. The actual value was 6 more than predicted#That would produce a positive residual of 6.
=C. The model overpredicted by 6#Correct. A negative residual means actual was less than predicted.
~D. The model cannot be interpreted#A residual can be interpreted as prediction error.




}

::ALG1_U06_L07_GP_Q05::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L07_GP_Q05] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.8]
Why might a simulation be useful?
{
~A. It proves the exact outcome of one event#Simulations estimate patterns; they do not prove a single outcome.
=B. It estimates a chance process by repeating trials#Correct. Repeated trials can estimate likely outcomes.
~C. It removes the need to define an event#The event must be defined before a simulation can be useful.
~D. It guarantees the population is unbiased#Simulation and sampling bias are different issues.




}
